					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-612633#post-1758646</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-612633/incident-log-1245-043#post-1758646</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Tue, 23 Apr 2013 15:53:10 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>chromed</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>1611390</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>It's been a while since I took chemistry, but I don't think hydrochloric acid is likely to cause a hull breach. Any hull material is going to be resistant to corrosion from saltwater and is most commonly either steel or PVC neither of which react significantly or quickly with HCl.</p> <p>Again, I may be wrong, but that segment should be researched further.</p> <p>Upon preliminary research: <a href="http://www.chemicalbook.com/ChemicalProductProperty_EN_CB7421538.htm">&quot;Corrodes galvanized or copper-alloy metals (brass, bronze); fittings of stainless steel or mild or cast steel must therefore be used.&quot;</a><br /> <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrochloric_acid#Chemical_properties_and_reactions">&quot;Personal protective equipment such as rubber or PVC gloves, protective eye goggles, and chemical-resistant clothing and shoes are used to minimize risks when handling hydrochloric acid.&quot;</a></p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
